    ":question/problem": "$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=$",
    ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
    ":question/worked-solution": "Here, the first factor is $4\\times 1$ matrix and the second factor is a $1\\times 3$ matrix. \n\nThere is $1$ column in the first matrix and $1$ row in the second matrix, so the product is well defined:\n\n$$\n4\\times \\boxed{\\text{1}}✓\\boxed{\\text{1}}\\times 3=4\\times 3\n$$\n\nComputing the product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 6 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0\\cdot 5 & 0\\cdot 6 & 0\\cdot 2 \\\\ (-6)\\cdot 5 & (-6)\\cdot 6 & (-6)\\cdot 2 \\\\ 1\\cdot 5 & 1\\cdot 6 & 1\\cdot 2 \\\\ 0\\cdot 5 & 0\\cdot 6 & 0\\cdot 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -30 & -36 & -12 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
